WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.870
<v Maya>Welcome to part five, the first part of the second lecture.

2
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:10.200
<v Maya>In lecture one we covered screening, the order of tests, blood pressure, and six body composition tests.

3
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:13.990
<v Maya>Two machines remain, underwater weighing and the DEXA scan.

4
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.190
<v Maya>Then we will pick the best of all eight.

5
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.210
<v Dev>So the numbering continues.

6
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:24.500
<v Dev>Underwater weighing is two point three point three, and DEXA is two point three point four.

7
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.380
<v Maya>Exactly.

8
00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:27.120
<v Maya>This test has three names.

9
00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:31.120
<v Maya>Hydrostatic test, hydro means water and static means still.

10
00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:35.240
<v Maya>Underwater weighing test, because the person's weight is measured underwater.

11
00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:39.970
<v Maya>And water displacement method, because water is displaced when the person goes in.

12
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.540
<v Maya>All the same test.

13
00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:45.210
<v Dev>And it's a densitometry method, like the Bod Pod?

14
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:46.100
<v Maya>Yes.

15
00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:49.320
<v Maya>Body density equals weight divided by volume.

16
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:55.120
<v Maya>Here the weight is checked underwater, with a special apparatus, and the volume comes from the water displaced.

17
00:00:55.170 --> 00:01:00.130
<v Maya>Then body density goes into the Siri equation to give body fat percentage.

18
00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.490
<v Dev>Where does Archimedes come in?

19
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.630
<v Maya>The method rests on his principle.

20
00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.820
<v Maya>Picture a tub filled to the brim and a box lowered in.

21
00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:09.300
<v Maya>Water spills out.

22
00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:14.320
<v Maya>Part one: the volume of the object underwater equals the volume of water displaced.

23
00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.600
<v Maya>That is how we get a person's volume.

24
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:22.410
<v Maya>Part two: an upward force acts on the object, the buoyant force, the push you feel in a swimming pool.

25
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.650
<v Maya>The water displaced is equivalent to the buoyant force.

26
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.730
<v Maya>So more volume, more water displaced, a higher buoyant force.

27
00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.990
<v Dev>Now the fat versus muscle bit.

28
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.900
<v Dev>Which takes more space, one kilogram of fat or one kilogram of muscle?

29
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:37.790
<v Maya>Fat.

30
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.600
<v Maya>Same weight, more volume.

31
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:40.970
<v Maya>So four points.

32
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.640
<v Maya>One, fat has more volume, muscle less.

33
00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:48.320
<v Maya>Two, fat displaces more water, muscle less, from Archimedes part one.

34
00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:52.200
<v Maya>Three, fat has higher buoyant force, muscle lower, from part two.

35
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:56.280
<v Maya>Four, fat is lighter than water, muscle is heavier, denser than water.

36
00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:00.200
<v Maya>That fourth point is a property of the tissues, not Archimedes.

37
00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.280
<v Maya>So fat tends to float and muscle tends to sink.

38
00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.600
<v Dev>A real person has both though.

39
00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:06.490
<v Maya>Right.

40
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.090
<v Maya>So nobody fully floats or fully sinks.

41
00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.810
<v Maya>It's a visual indicator.

42
00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.360
<v Maya>More tendency to float suggests more fat and less muscle.

43
00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.710
<v Maya>The slides show the same for two people.

44
00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:23.490
<v Maya>Low body fat percentage means less volume, less water displaced, less buoyant, heavier, and weighs more in water.

45
00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:29.510
<v Maya>High body fat percentage means more volume, more displacement, more buoyant, and weighs less in water.

46
00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.420
<v Dev>Give me a memory hook.

47
00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.240
<v Maya>Fat is fluffy and floats.

48
00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:35.290
<v Maya>Muscle is muscly and sinks.

49
00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:41.930
<v Maya>This level of detail is why hydrostatic weighing is called the gold standard of body composition tests.

50
00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:45.870
<v Maya>It has the highest accuracy, and other tests are compared against it.

51
00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.320
<v Maya>Remember the skinfold with its plus or minus three percent.

52
00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.990
<v Maya>The gold standard is far more reliable.

53
00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.070
<v Dev>Quick check.

54
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.300
<v Dev>Which two parts of Archimedes, in my words?

55
00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:03.150
<v Dev>Volume underwater equals water displaced, and water displaced equals the upward buoyant force.

56
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:03.900
<v Maya>Perfect.

57
00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.470
<v Maya>In the demonstration video from a university laboratory, two measurements are needed.

58
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:13.260
<v Maya>Body mass, easy on a scale, and body volume, which is the whole point of the tank.

59
00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:15.930
<v Maya>A scale is connected by a rod to a seat.

60
00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:20.470
<v Maya>The client sits on the seat, the water level is at the neck, and she goes underwater.

61
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.730
<v Dev>Why does she have to blow out air?

62
00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.360
<v Maya>Two things make a person float, fat and air.

63
00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.950
<v Maya>The air in the lungs would distort the reading.

64
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:35.800
<v Maya>So the client blows out all the air underwater, then a little more, and stays as still as possible, because movement makes the needle hard to read.

65
00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.760
<v Maya>The tester watches the bubbles.

66
00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:41.300
<v Maya>When they stop, the air is gone and the reading is taken.

67
00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:43.270
<v Dev>And one try is enough?

68
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.860
<v Maya>No, several trials, so the reading is the same each time.

69
00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:51.450
<v Maya>A single trial may be wrong if the client moved or didn't exhale properly.

70
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.080
<v Dev>That sounds hard.

71
00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.060
<v Maya>It is.

72
00:03:54.110 --> 00:04:01.330
<v Maya>Staying underwater without breathing is hard, and exhaling underwater is unnatural, because instinctively we hold our breath.

73
00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.530
<v Maya>Someone afraid of water can't do it.

74
00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:06.200
<v Maya>That's why the Bod Pod suits more people.

75
00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:07.940
<v Dev>Pros and cons?

76
00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:09.920
<v Maya>One advantage, accuracy.

77
00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:14.720
<v Maya>Disadvantages: it needs technical expertise, the operator must be qualified.

78
00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:18.030
<v Maya>It's expensive, the skilled operator plus the whole set up.

79
00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.660
<v Maya>And it isn't available to the general public, or in India, as the board says.

80
00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:27.170
<v Dev>And you wouldn't call it the best test if the client has to travel abroad.

81
00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:30.300
<v Maya>Exactly, affordability and availability matter.

82
00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:34.420
<v Dev>A student asked about the accuracy of the Siri equation.

83
00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:39.070
<v Maya>The answer: accuracy is judged in how accurately body density is measured.

84
00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.470
<v Maya>The gold standard measures density very accurately, and the Siri equation is well tested.

85
00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.730
<v Maya>Some small error always exists.

86
00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:50.730
<v Maya>Pause and think: why is accuracy about what goes into the equation?

87
00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.720
<v Maya>Because a good formula can't fix a bad measurement.

88
00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:54.780
<v Maya>Right?

89
00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:55.670
<v Dev>Right.

90
00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.450
<v Dev>Garbage in, garbage out.

91
00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:01.520
<v Maya>Now DEXA, Dual Energy X-ray Absorptiometry.

92
00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:05.910
<v Maya>Dual means two, and the two are a low energy beam and a high energy beam.

93
00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:09.530
<v Maya>Spelling trap: it is absorptiometry, not absorption.

94
00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:14.160
<v Maya>The final n of absorption is missing, and exams can use that as a trap.

95
00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:15.740
<v Dev>And the principle?

96
00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:17.840
<v Maya>Density decides absorption.

97
00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:20.980
<v Maya>Bone is high density, muscle moderate, fat low.

98
00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:24.030
<v Maya>The higher the density, the more radiation absorbed.

99
00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.900
<v Maya>Bone absorbs very well, so little passes through.

100
00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:29.620
<v Maya>Fat absorbs poorly, so most passes through.

101
00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.330
<v Maya>Muscle sits between.

102
00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:37.870
<v Maya>The machine analyses how much was absorbed and how much passed through, and works out how much of each tissue there is.

103
00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.430
<v Maya>That is also how the colour coded body image is made, like a weather map.

104
00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.430
<v Dev>Fit this into the two categories we learned.

105
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:49.250
<v Maya>Earlier we split body weight into lean body mass and body fat.

106
00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:54.440
<v Maya>Bod Pod and underwater weighing also gave only two, fat mass and fat free mass.

107
00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:57.430
<v Maya>DEXA goes one level further, with three.

108
00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:00.290
<v Maya>Fat free mass is muscle, organs and fluid.

109
00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:04.410
<v Maya>Fat mass is subcutaneous, visceral and essential fat.

110
00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:08.150
<v Maya>And bone and mineral mass, which gets a category of its own.

111
00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.680
<v Dev>So is fat free mass the same as lean body mass?

112
00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:13.180
<v Maya>Not exactly here.

113
00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:17.390
<v Maya>In the two category view, lean mass includes bone and essential fat.

114
00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:23.640
<v Maya>In DEXA, bone is separate and essential fat is counted as fat, so fat free mass is narrower.

115
00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:29.310
<v Maya>So the two lectures use these terms a little differently, and your course material is the final word.

116
00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.700
<v Dev>Where did DEXA come from?

117
00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.700
<v Maya>Originally orthopaedics.

118
00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:36.240
<v Maya>Doctors used it to check bone density.

119
00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:42.040
<v Maya>Now it's used for body composition because it separates fat, fat free mass and bone so neatly.

120
00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:43.540
<v Dev>Advantages?

121
00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:50.600
<v Maya>Low radiation risk, no special preparation, no fasting like BIA, no special clothing like the Bod Pod.

122
00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.270
<v Maya>Good accuracy, compared with the gold standard.

123
00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:54.160
<v Maya>Quick.

124
00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:58.480
<v Maya>No technical expertise needed for the user, lab technicians run it.

125
00:06:58.530 --> 00:06:59.840
<v Maya>Suitable for all.

126
00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:01.390
<v Maya>Available in India.

127
00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:02.990
<v Maya>In depth analysis.

128
00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:06.210
<v Maya>One disadvantage: it might be expensive for some.

129
00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:07.820
<v Dev>How expensive?

130
00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:15.100
<v Maya>About three thousand to four thousand at a public facility, and twelve thousand to fifteen thousand at a premium private hospital.

131
00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.910
<v Maya>The currency wasn't stated, taken as rupees.

132
00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:21.280
<v Maya>Explain it to a client as an investment, not an overhead.

133
00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:25.330
<v Maya>It's not a monthly cost, you can repeat it after about six months.

134
00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:28.740
<v Dev>What about the gym's entry level BIA machine?

135
00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:38.150
<v Maya>The practical plan from the lecture: get a DEXA, compare it with the BIA reading, and if they differ by about plus or minus five percent, you know the margin.

136
00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.930
<v Maya>Then repeat the DEXA after six months.

137
00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:51.460
<v Maya>For your course note the sources word DEXA accuracy differently: the slide says as accurate as hydrostatic weighing, the E-book says more valid than a two component model.

138
00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:52.980
<v Maya>Now the big question.

139
00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:54.850
<v Maya>Which of the eight is the best?

140
00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:56.940
<v Maya>The first filter is validity.

141
00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:02.000
<v Maya>A test is valid for body composition only if it gives a body fat percentage.

142
00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:03.690
<v Dev>So walk me through.

143
00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:05.800
<v Maya>Step one, anthropometry.

144
00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:10.310
<v Maya>Waist circumference, waist to hip ratio and BMI give no percentage.

145
00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:11.420
<v Maya>Not valid.

146
00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:18.890
<v Maya>Step two, skinfold gives a percentage, so valid, but not reliable, because it covers only subcutaneous fat.

147
00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:25.020
<v Maya>Step three, of the machines, underwater weighing and Bod Pod are not available in India, so they are out.

148
00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.720
<v Maya>Step four, BIA against DEXA.

149
00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:33.580
<v Maya>DEXA wins, with in depth analysis and accuracy compared against the gold standard.

150
00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:36.710
<v Dev>So DEXA is the most recommended.

151
00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.020
<v Maya>Yes, considering all factors.

152
00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:39.970
<v Maya>Recap.

153
00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.100
<v Maya>Underwater weighing is gold standard but hard to get.

154
00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:47.280
<v Maya>DEXA uses two beams, three categories, and is available.

155
00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:48.640
<v Dev>Pause and think.

156
00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:53.390
<v Dev>Why isn't underwater weighing chosen, even though it's the gold standard?

157
00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:54.800
<v Maya>What's your answer?

158
00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:59.490
<v Dev>Because it needs experts, it's expensive, and it's not available in India.

159
00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:00.520
<v Maya>Correct.

160
00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:01.830
<v Maya>Here are the five.

161
00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:07.980
<v Maya>One, Archimedes: volume equals water displaced, and displaced water equals buoyant force.

162
00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:12.090
<v Maya>Two, fat is lighter than water and floats, muscle is denser and sinks.

163
00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:17.310
<v Maya>Three, underwater weighing is the gold standard, but needs expertise and isn't available.

164
00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:25.290
<v Maya>Four, DEXA uses two beams, the denser the tissue the more it absorbs, and reports fat free mass, fat mass and bone mass.

165
00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:34.370
<v Maya>Five, anthropometry is not valid, skinfold not reliable, underwater weighing and Bod Pod not available, so DEXA is the choice.

166
00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:36.770
<v Dev>Next, the heart and lung tests.

167
00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:38.110
<v Maya>See you in part six.

